From 9ad4a1fdb8a2538f477f5d6cdf41bf29bd5da5ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 21:12:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 504/507] [DS4.1] Routed experts: 64-row tiles for prefill
 calls

Every grouped-GEMM tile holds block_M routes of one expert and reads that
expert's weight slab again, so at prefill sizes, where an expert has hundreds
of routes, the 32-row decode tile makes the routed experts L2-bound on weight
re-reads. A call now takes 64-row tiles once the average expert has a full
32-row tile of routes (rows * topk >= 32 * experts: 2,048 tokens for V4.1's
384 experts at top-6); decode steps and their CUDA graphs (at most 96 rows),
the CED decoder's compacted rows and the drafter's MoE stay at 32. The plan
declares both heights' GEMMs, fused SwiGLU and dispatch, and lays each call's
scratch out at its own height (the layout pads to 64 rows: about 0.16 GiB more
workspace at TP3's 8,192 tokens). A call's rows are its routes' rows
(topk_ids), the tokens arriving as their producer's MXFP8 rows.

A row's sum over K runs in the same order at either tile height, so every
output bit is unchanged: the tests compare the two heights through the whole
plan near and above the threshold, the fused SwiGLU against TileKernels at
64-row tiles, and the existing batch-composition checks now cross the two
heights. GB10, one layer's experts (spark-ds41f
experiments/2026-10-26-tp3-budget-memory): -11% at 8,192 tokens and -19% at
16,384 at TP3's 768 columns a rank, -11% and -15% at TP4's 576.
benchmark_deepseek_v41_moe_prefill_tiles.py times the plan as serving calls
it against the plan before it (the parent commit's moe.py), on one shared
copy of the weights and tokens, gated on identical outputs, never slower
where the heights differ, and faster from 8,192 tokens.
